# Citation File Format — https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# GitHub reads this and renders a "Cite this repository" button on the repo page,
# offering APA and BibTeX. That is the point of the file: it turns "who made this"
# from something you dig out of git log into something the platform surfaces.
#
# `version` is a COPY of the VERSION file, so tests/test_version_ssot.py asserts the
# two agree. Same reason deploy/model-flags.conf and the Node pin carry guards: this
# repo has been bitten repeatedly by one moving number restated in several places,
# and a stale citation is worse than none because people paste it verbatim.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Ava, build on it, or write about it, please cite it as below."
title: "Ava: a private, self-hosted AI assistant that runs on your own hardware"
abstract: >-
  Ava is a self-hosted personal AI assistant: chat, voice, images and agentic code
  changes, running entirely on hardware you own. It bundles an inference engine, an
  agent runtime in a sandbox with deny-by-default egress policy, a connector SDK for
  wiring in your own applications, and an operations dashboard that reports what it
  has actually measured — including a data page that lists every store it keeps, with
  audited deletes and a destruction path for biometric voiceprints.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Thompson
    given-names: Joshua
    alias: JayMTea
    # No email on purpose: this file is published and scraped. The GitHub profile is
    # the reachable route, and it is the same one NOTICE names.
repository-code: "https://github.com/JayMTea/Ava"
url: "https://jaymtea.github.io/Ava/"
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.1.0
keywords:
  - self-hosted
  - personal-ai
  - llm
  - voice-assistant
  - agent
  - privacy
  - on-device
  - homelab
